w shares. He can sell them to pay the tax. In return, he will stay in California for eight years.\n\nPeople in California vote on the tax next month. It is 5% for about 250 billionaires. Many tech billionaires do not like it.\n\nMeta also said its profits fell. Its shares went down 6% on Thursday.", "body_zh": "扎克伯格非常有钱。他说他可能搬出加州。这项新税可能让他花很多钱。\n\n所以Meta会给他新的股票。他可以卖掉股票来交税。作为交换，他会在加州住八年。\n\n加州人下个月对这项税投票。税率是5%，针对大约250个亿万富翁。很多科技富豪不喜欢它。\n\nMeta还说它的利润下降了。周四它的股价跌了6%。", "text": "Zuckerberg is very rich. He said he may move out of California. The new tax could cost him a lot of money. So Meta will give him new shares. He can sell them to pay the tax. In return, he will stay in California for eight years. People in California vote on the tax next month. It is 5% for about 250 billionaires. Many tech billionaires do not like it. Meta also said its profits fell. Its shares went down 6% on Thursday.", "vocab": [{"word": "tax", "pos": "noun", "zh": "税", "note": "常和pay连用：pay a tax", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "rich", "pos": "adj", "zh": "有钱的", "note": "very rich 非常有钱", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "move", "pos": "verb", "zh": "搬家；搬走", "note": "move out of 搬出", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "share", "pos": "noun", "zh": "股票；股份", "note": "常用复数 shares", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "sell", "pos": "verb", "zh": "卖", "note": "过去式 sold", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "stay", "pos": "verb", "zh": "留下；待着", "note": "stay in a place 留在某地", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "vote", "pos": "verb", "zh": "投票", "note": "vote on 对……投票", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "profit", "pos": "noun", "zh": "利润", "note": "常用复数 profits", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Meta strikes deal with Mark Zuckerberg to help him foot billionaire tax bill", "summary_original": "Chief executive agrees to remain in California for eight years even if state brings in one-off 5% wealth tax\nMeta has struck a deal with Mark Zuckerberg to help him foot a possible California wealth tax targeting billionaires.\nThe social media company behind Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp revealed on Wednesday night that the arrangement would keep its chief executive living in California, where Meta’s headquarters are located.\nContinue reading...", "body_original": "Meta strikes deal with Mark Zuckerberg to help him foot billionaire tax bill\nChief executive agrees to remain in California for eight years even if state brings in one-off 5% wealth tax\nMeta has struck a deal with\nMark Zuckerberg\nto help him foot a possible California wealth tax targeting billionaires.\nThe social media company behind Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp revealed on Wednesday night that the arrangement would keep its chief executive living in\nCalifornia\n, where Meta’s headquarters are located.\nUnder the deal, Zuckerberg will receive new shares that could be sold to potentially foot a multibillion-dollar tax bill in the event that California passes a one-off billionaire tax. In return, he is committed to remain a resident of the state, or wherever\nMeta\nmaintains its base, for eight years.\nCalifornians will vote early next month on Proposition 40, a one-off 5% tax on the roughly 250 billionaires who live in the state.\nZuckerberg, worth $239bn (£180bn) according to Forbes, told Meta he was considering moving his primary residence elsewhere because Prop 40 could expose him to a massive tax liability, potentially forcing him to sell voting shares to pay it.\nIn response, Meta is creating a new class of non-voting shares via a two-for-one stock split for all existing shareholders. This will allow Zuckerberg to sell some of these new class C shares to pay a potential tax bill, without diluting his control of the company.\nRecent polling has shown that Prop 40 remains short of majority support with voters. Tech billionaires\nhave been spending heavily to fight the measure\n, including the Google co-founder Sergey Brin, the Palantir co-founder Peter Thiel and the former Google CEO Eric Schmidt.\nRohit Khanna, the US congressman representing California’s 17th congressional district, has described Prop 40 as a choice between standing with the working class or the oligarchs.\n“Every society is taxed. The only question is who will pay,”\nKhanna wrote in the Guardian last week\n.\n“In Trump’s America, working people pay more than they should, while billionaires are so unburdened that they are becoming trillionaires. In the America we seek, billionaires and trillionaires will pay their fair share, while working people will be unburdened.”\nMeta also reported a 26% rise in revenues for the last quarter to $64.6bn, beating expectations of $63.3bn. But profits fell by 37% to $13bn, dragged down by a $9.9bn charge related to Meta’s\nlawsuit over child protection and social media addiction\nfailings.\nThe company’s shares slid by more than 6% when trading opened on Wall Street on Thursday, helping to drag the tech-focused Nasdaq index down 2.5% amid a wider selloff of AI-linked stocks.\nIn a separate case, on Wednesday a judge in New Mexico\nordered Meta to pay $25bn\nfollowing a trial that found the company had misled the public about how it handled Facebook user data.\nMeta also revealed that it had cut its headcount to 66,775 at the end of September, 15% fewer than a year ago.\nReuters contributed to this report.\nExplore more on these topics\nMeta\nMark Zuckerberg\nCalifornia\nWest Coast\nThe super-rich\nnews\nShare\nReuse this content", "fetched_at": "2026-10-23T23:57:30.310630", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Zuckerberg", "s": 0.325, "e": 0.9, "sm": 325, "em": 899, "cs": 0, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "is", "s": 0.9, "e": 1.05, "sm": 900, "em": 1050, "cs": 11, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "very", "s": 1.05, "e": 1.387, "sm": 1050, "em": 1387, "cs": 14, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "rich", "s": 1.387, "e": 1.925, "sm": 1387, "em": 1924, "cs": 19, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": ".", "s": 1.925, "e": 2.025, "sm": 1925, 